- How would you handle non-monotone constraints or introduce probabilistic uncertainty in truth values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the backtracking algorithm for larger sensor networks?
- Can you extend this approach to handle additional logical operators?
- What strategies would you use to minimize the number of U values in the final assignment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests concrete application of 3-valued logic to pipeline gating, not just theory. Requires constructing a satisfiable partial assignment and a propagation strategy that avoids prematurely forcing F.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F ordering; ¬, ∧, ∨, → semantics
- Backtracking/forward-chaining under partial info
- Satisfiability with Unknowns in real-world policy constraints

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Kleene-like 3-valued evaluator for our constraints
const V = {T:2, U:1, F:0};
function not(x){ return x===V.T?V.F : x===V.F?V.T : V.U; }
function or(a,b){ return a>b? a:b; }
function and(a,b){ return a<b? a:b; }
function imp(a,b){ // a -> b is (!a) OR b
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

